Do you want the flag to spin on its own centre, or to travel around a circle like a moon? To make it spin in place, set the element to inline-block, give it a size, and add an animation that rotates it steadily from zero to three hundred sixty degrees.

```html
<span class="spin">🇨🇳</span>

<style>
.spin {
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 64px;
  animation: spin 2s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
</style>
```

Use linear timing so the speed stays constant through each turn, and change the duration to make it faster or slower.

If you mean orbiting instead, place the flag inside a relative container and centre it there. Then animate it by rotating the container, pushing the flag out by ninety pixels to set the radius, and rotating the flag back so it stays upright. Leave out that final rotation if you prefer it to tumble.

```html
<div class="stage">
  <span class="orbit">🇨🇳</span>
</div>

<style>
.stage {
  position: relative;
  width: 240px;
  height: 240px;
}

.orbit {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  font-size: 40px;
  margin: -20px 0 0 -20px;
  animation: orbit 3s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes orbit {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg) translateX(90px) rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg) translateX(90px) rotate(-360deg); }
}
</style>
```

One thing to know is that Windows does not draw flag emojis, so there the symbol will appear as the letters CN. If that matters, use an image of the flag with the same class, and it will animate in exactly the same way.<|im_end|>

